course: biology
lesson: 387
title: Clever primates: tool-using chimps, Kanzi the bonobo and Ayumu's memory
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A chimpanzee picks a grass stem, strips off the leaves, pokes it into a termite mound, waits, and pulls it out covered in termites. Stripping the leaves means it is making a tool, not just finding one.
explain: Young chimps learn tricks like this by watching their mothers for years, the way you learned to tie your laces by watching someone.
explain: Different chimp groups use different tools, and snow monkeys in Japan copied one young female who washed sandy sweet potatoes. A habit learned from others and passed on is a small piece of culture.
explain: Some apes learned surprising things with people: Kanzi the bonobo used hundreds of picture symbols, and Ayumu the chimp remembered numbers flashed on a screen for less than a second.
explain: But scientists must test carefully, because it is easy to see what we hope to see in an animal's answers.
fact: A chimp pushes a stripped grass stem into a termite mound, waits for termites to bite it and pulls it out to eat them.
fact: Chimps in West Africa crack nuts by putting them on a stone or root anvil and hitting them with a hammer stone; it takes years of practice.
fact: Scientists dug up chimp nut-cracking stones about 4,300 years old.
fact: Chimps also use chewed-leaf sponges and digging sticks, and some in Senegal were reported to sharpen sticks to hunt small animals in tree holes.
fact: Kanzi the bonobo learned hundreds of picture symbols by watching his mother's lessons, understood many spoken English sentences and learned to make sharp stone flakes.
fact: Ayumu, a young chimp in Japan, remembered where numbers 1 to 9 were after seeing them for less than a second; whether chimps really beat people at this is still argued.
fact: In Japan a young female macaque began washing sandy sweet potatoes in water, and the habit spread through her troop by copying.
fact: Bearded capuchin monkeys in Brazil crack palm nuts with stones nearly as heavy as themselves, and they choose and carry the best stones.
fact: Orangutans, which live in the rainforests of Borneo and Sumatra, use big leaves as umbrellas, and one wild orangutan was reported to put chewed plant juice on a cut on his face, which then healed.
fact: Apes have five fingers on each hand and five toes on each foot, with a grasping big toe; a chimp's thumb is shorter than ours.
fact: Apes have no tails, while most monkeys have them.
fact: Koko the gorilla used many signs, but scientists still argue how much she understood, so careful tests are needed.
exercise: What does a chimp do to a grass stem before termite fishing? | Strips the leaves off it | Paints it all over with wet mud | Sets it on fire to make it hard | Ties it in a tight knot first
exercise: How do young chimps learn to fish for termites? | By watching their mothers for years | They are born knowing how | By reading signs scratched on trees | People in the forest train every one of them
exercise: What do West African chimps use to crack nuts? | A hammer stone and an anvil | Their back teeth only | A metal nutcracker they stole from a camp | Sharp sticks
exercise: How old are the oldest chimp nut-cracking stones found? | About 4,300 years | Only about 43 years | About 43 million years | Only about 4 years
exercise: What do chimps use chewed leaves for? | As a sponge to soak up water | As a hat to keep off the rain | As a soft warm blanket for babies | As food to feed to the termites
exercise: Why might two chimp groups use different tools? | Each group learns its own habits from others, like culture | Each group is a different species of chimp with different hands | Tools grow in different trees | One group is always cleverer
exercise: How did Kanzi learn his picture symbols? | By watching his mother's lessons | From a book | By being punished | From another bonobo in the wild forest of Congo
exercise: What did Kanzi learn to make? | Sharp stone flakes to cut a rope | Bread and cakes for his keepers | A wooden boat | Paper aeroplanes
exercise: What did Ayumu do in his famous test? | Touched numbers 1 to 9 in order after a quick flash | Counted all the way to a thousand out loud in Japanese | Wrote his own name | Solved a jigsaw of the world
exercise: How did potato washing spread among Japanese macaques? | Others in the troop copied one young female | The keepers taught every single monkey by hand | They were all born doing it | It came from the sea itself
exercise: What do bearded capuchins in Brazil use to crack palm nuts? | Big stones nearly as heavy as themselves | Their sharp claws | Old car tyres they find by the roads | Pointed sticks
exercise: What do orangutans use a big leaf for in the rain? | As an umbrella | As a plate for food | As a drum to bang on | As a fan to cool down
exercise: What was reported about one wild orangutan's wound? | He put chewed plant juice on it and it healed | He covered it with mud forever | He asked a vet for help at the rescue centre | He ignored it and it got worse
exercise: How many fingers and toes does a chimp have in all? | Twenty | Only ten | Sixteen | Twenty-four
exercise: Why can't a chimp hold a pencil as neatly as you? | Its thumb is shorter than ours | It has no thumbs at all on its hands | Its fingers are made of soft rubber | It has only three fingers on each hand
exercise: Why must scientists test talking apes carefully? | It is easy to see what we hope to see in their answers | Apes always lie | Apes cannot see the signs well | Testing makes apes cleverer each time, so the answers keep on changing
test: «chimps just find sticks, that ain't making tools» What is right? | They strip the leaves first, so they shape a tool | Yes, they never change the stick in any way at all | Chimps cannot hold sticks | Only people make tools
test: Count them: Curious George is called a monkey but has no tail. A real chimp has how many tails? | None, apes have no tails | One, like all monkeys | Two | One, but very short and curly
test: Count them: 3 chimps. How many fingers and toes in all? | Sixty | Thirty | Forty-eight | Twenty
test: In The Jungle Book King Louie is an orangutan king in India. What is right about real orangutans? | They live in the forests of Borneo and Sumatra, not India | They rule all the monkeys of India from an old ruined temple | They live in deserts | They cannot climb
test: What would happen if a young chimp grew up with no adults to watch? | It would find it much harder to learn termite fishing and nut cracking | It would know every trick anyway | It would teach the adults | It would learn every trick faster on its own, because nobody would get in its way
test: «my mate says the chimp beat students at memory, so chimps are cleverer than people» What is the fair view? | Ayumu did better in one quick-memory test; that is argued and is not all of cleverness | Yes, chimps are cleverer than people at everything, from maths to building houses and cars | No, Ayumu failed the test | The students were asleep
test: «why'd a monkey in Brazil and a chimp in Africa both use stones, did they copy?» What is right? | They worked it out separately, because stones are good for cracking hard nuts | The chimps sailed to Brazil | The monkeys taught the chimps by phone | They are the same animal, only living in two places far apart
test: «Koko knew sign language so she talked like us, done» What is the careful view? | She used many signs, but how much she understood is still argued | She spoke English out loud | She wrote books | She knew nothing at all and only copied her keeper's hands for food
test: «why don't my big toes grab stuff like a chimp's» What is the best answer? | Our feet are made for walking, theirs for climbing and gripping | We forgot how to use them because we stopped climbing trees as babies | Shoes squashed them | Chimps have no big toes
test: How could you test whether an ape really understands a word? | Have someone who does not know the answer ask in new situations | Ask the ape's own keeper what it means, since the keeper knows the ape best | Show it the answer first | Count how often it smiles
test: «snow monkeys washing potatoes, that's just instinct» What do scientists say? | It was a new habit copied from one young female, a piece of culture | All monkeys are born washing food, just as they are born knowing how to climb | Keepers trained each one | It only happened in a film
exam: Identify the principal reason for which termite fishing by chimpanzees is regarded as tool manufacture. | The animal modifies the stem by stripping its leaves prior to use | The stem is employed without any alteration | The termites themselves construct the implement for the chimpanzee | The behaviour is confined to captive animals
exam: Ascertain the aggregate number of fingers and toes possessed by a single chimpanzee. | Twenty | Ten | Sixteen | Eighteen
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the washing of sweet potatoes by Japanese macaques. | The habit began with one young female and spread through the troop by imitation | It was instilled in each individual by human keepers | It is an innate behaviour of all monkeys | It was observed exclusively in a laboratory
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the scientific assessment of signing by the gorilla Koko? | Her use of signs is acknowledged, but the extent of her comprehension remains disputed | She has been conclusively demonstrated to speak aloud | She is agreed by all scientists to have understood every sign without exception | She is agreed to have understood no sign whatsoever
